{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-269557",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "The total derivative of a vector-valued function $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{m}$ with respect to a vector $x\\in {R}^{n}$ is the $m\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}]\\in {R}^{m\\times n}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, writing out the function $f(x)$ where $x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\in {R}^{2}$, we get\n\n$$\nf(x)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 5 \\\\ 6 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} \\\\ 3{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} \\\\ 4{x}_{1}+5{x}_{2} \\\\ 6{x}_{1}+5{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, the total derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂{x}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{4}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{4}}{∂{x}_{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}({x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}({x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}(3{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}(3{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}(4{x}_{1}+5{x}_{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}(4{x}_{1}+5{x}_{2}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}(6{x}_{1}+5{x}_{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}(6{x}_{1}+5{x}_{2})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 5 \\\\ 6 & 5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the total derivative is a constant matrix, evaluating it at ${x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]$ gives the same matrix.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
